  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1208 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nWeakening, elongated closed midlevel cyclone was centered over\nUtah and Arizona at midday, per water vapor satellite imagery.\nRadar imagery at noon depicted widespread rain moving north\nacross the western half of SW KS. Moist conveyor belt ahead of\nthe stationary Rockies trough is well established, with radar\nimagery from Amarillo showing rain stacked up across the Texas\npanhandle moving north. Surface analysis depicted a very obvious\nstationary frontal boundary, draped southeast of DDC across the\nsoutheast CWA. The moist conveyor belt overrunning this surface\nboundary will enhance rainfall generation through tonight, as\nthe front is forced back northward, and winds return to\nsoutherly. Lightning detection depicted some strikes near\nAmarillo at midday, but instability was absent over SW KS.\nInstability will remain weak through Monday night, with forecast\nCAPE < 500 J/kg through the period. Thunder from the NBM \ngenerated grids was accepted, but should be very limited. \nStorm total QPF grids through Monday continue to show a strong \ngradient in forecast rainfall amounts, from near zero far \neastern zones, ranging to 1-2 inches west of US 283. \n\nTrough over the Rockies Sunday will eject northeast into the\ncentral Plains 7 pm Monday. At least scattered rain showers will\npersist through Monday as trailing shortwaves and lingering lift\nkeep some showers going. Lingering cloud cover will be\nsignificant Monday, and NBM afternoon temperatures in the lower\n70s are expected to be several degrees too warm. 12z MOS\nguidance in the 60s looks more realistic, despite winds\nacquiring some SWly downslope component in the afternoon. \n\nStrong subsidence will finally end all shower activity and clear\nthe sky Tuesday. A warming trend well through the 70s to near 80\nis expected Wednesday, and with elevated SWly downslope and full\nsunshine, NBM may not be warm enough. A mild late October will\ncontinue, with no cold air intrusions noted, with high\nconfidence both sunrise and afternoon temperatures will be well\nabove normal, with no freezes, through Halloween. Expect SW KS \nto remain dry Wednesday and Thursday, however medium range \nmodels, especially the 12z GFS and its ensemble members, show a \nstrong subtropical moisture push northward out of West Texas on\nFriday. With similar trends from ECMWF ensembles, NBM pops in \nthe likely to definite category are already in place Friday \nafternoon/evening, favoring the southeast counties.",
